The powerset of the naturals is a neighbourhood system whose neighbourhoods are the cofinite complements of finite sets of bits.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`nbhd`).** The neighbourhood coded by a natural number \(n\) consists of the bit positions at which \(n\) has value false.

**Theorem (`mem_nbhd`).** A natural number \(k\) belongs to the neighbourhood coded by \(n\) exactly when the \(k\)-th bit of \(n\) is false.

*Proof.* This is the defining membership condition. ∎

**Theorem (`nbhd_injective`).** Distinct natural-number codes determine distinct neighbourhoods.

*Proof.* Equality of the neighbourhoods gives equality of the truth value of every bit, and natural numbers are determined by their bits. ∎

**Theorem (`nbhd_inter`).** The intersection of the neighbourhoods coded by \(n\) and \(m\) is the neighbourhood coded by their bitwise disjunction.

*Proof.* A bit is false in the disjunction exactly when it is false in both operands. ∎

**Theorem (`nbhd_zero`).** The neighbourhood coded by zero is the whole set of natural numbers.

*Proof.* Every bit of zero is false. ∎

**Definition (`PN`).** The powerset neighbourhood system on the natural numbers has precisely the coded neighbourhoods, with the whole set as master neighbourhood.

*Proof.* Zero codes the master neighbourhood, and bitwise disjunction codes intersections. ∎

**Definition (`PNpres`).** The canonical computable presentation enumerates the neighbourhood coded by \(n\) at index \(n\), uses bitwise disjunction for intersection, and uses zero for the master index.

*Proof.* Injectivity of the coding decides equality, bitwise disjunction computes intersections, and every pair of neighbourhoods is consistent. ∎

**Theorem (`PN_consistent`).** Any two coded neighbourhoods have a coded neighbourhood contained in their intersection.

*Proof.* Their bitwise disjunction codes the intersection itself. ∎

**Theorem (`PN_isEffectivelyGiven`).** The powerset neighbourhood system is effectively given.

*Proof.* The canonical computable presentation supplies the required effective enumeration and operations. ∎
